## Scribase backend

This project's backend runs on **Scribase** (Supabase-compatible Postgres,
Auth, Storage, Realtime, Edge Functions). App code uses
`@supabase/supabase-js` against the project URL and anon key in `.env`.
Management (projects, environments, migrations, keys, branches) goes through
the Scribase MCP server (tools such as `project.put`, `environment.create`,
`migrate.run`) or the `scribase` CLI. Full guide:
https://docs.scribase.com/llms.txt

Signing in (never ask the user for a key):

- Never ask the user for API keys, `service_role` / secret keys, database
  passwords or personal access tokens. Nobody needs to paste a secret.
- To authenticate, run `scribase login --json` (or `npx scribase login
  --json`). It prints a line with `verification_uri_complete` and `user_code`:
  show the user that link and code, ask them to approve in the browser, and
  wait for the final line (`"ok":true`). The token is stored for you (OS
  keychain or `~/.config/scribase/credentials`) and never printed. If your
  shell only shows output when a command ends, run `scribase login --json
  --no-wait`, show the link, then run `scribase login --json` again after the
  user approves.
- No account yet, or a throwaway backend: `npx scribase init --temp --yes
  --json` (a 72-hour project with a claim link). `npx scribase init --yes
  --json` signs in through the same browser approval when needed.
- Claude Code: `claude mcp add --transport http scribase
  https://api.scribase.com/mcp` (browser sign-in on first connect, no key).
- A token starting with `scb_agt_` is an agent token, designed to be shared
  with coding agents: scoped, expiring, revocable and audited. If the user
  pastes one, use it: put it in `SCRIBASE_ACCESS_TOKEN` or the gitignored env
  file, never commit or echo it. The part before the last `_` summarizes it
  (`1p-rw-sqlw-mig-x2609260412` = one project, read/write, SQL write,
  migrations, expires 2026-09-26 04:12 UTC).
- If the user pastes anything else that looks secret (a `service_role` or
  `sb_secret_` key, a database password or connection string, an `scb_pat_`
  or `sbk_` token), do not repeat it or use it; tell them to revoke or rotate
  it and run `scribase login` instead.

Rules:

- Never print, log or commit keys or tokens. Write them to `.env` (gitignored)
  and refer to them by name. Only the anon/publishable key goes in client code;
  `service_role` / `sb_secret_` keys are server-only.
- Schema changes are numbered SQL files in `supabase/migrations/`, applied with
  `scribase migrate <org> <project> <env> --dir supabase/migrations` (plan)
  then `--apply`. Never run ad-hoc DDL against production.
- Every new table enables row level security with owner policies in the same
  migration. Regenerate types afterwards with `scribase gen types`.
- Work on a preview branch per task (`scribase env create ... preview
  sanitized ...` + `scribase branch register ... --base production`). Merging
  into production, deleting environments or projects, rotating keys and
  restores need the user's explicit approval for that specific operation.
- MCP mutations refuse without `confirm: true` and return the exact request;
  show it to the user when it touches production. `database.query` is
  read-only by design.
- Lifecycle calls return an operation: poll `scribase operation get` (MCP
  `operation.get`) until `succeeded`.
- Use `scribase help` or the MCP tool list for the exact surface; never invent
  commands, flags or endpoints.
